# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses. The locale entries come
first, the easiest at the top: fixes of a line or two, then fixes that
take work, then the entries that wait on a decision. The extension
sweep's findings follow, the smallest decision first: dead code and
stale comments, then behaviour the library adds or changes, then the
additions whose removal costs tests or callers. The rest, outside the
locale, is last.

** TODO Sweep the library for extensions

Done 2026-11-04 by four readings of =include= and =src= against
14882:2003, one per area: the containers, iterators, algorithms,
utilities and numerics, with =include/tr1=; the strings and streams;
the locale; and clauses 17 to 19 with the C headers and Annex D. The
negative precision under =fixed= had left the same day (9a5a402a).
Four defects the readings found were queued the same day, confirmed by
probe (845a7d93). The findings follow this entry: 43 entries, the
smallest decision first; the 17 defects and hypotheses seen in passing
among the entries outside the locale follow the locale entries.
Documentation has 5 more, on the reference pages and the record, and
Tests 1. The sources carry 67 comments that say "extension": 56 mark an
addition of the library (the containers' area 14, the streams' 12, the
locale's 21, the support clauses' 9), and 11 name a compiler's or the C
library's extension, a sign extension or a reserved field. One extension
stays by decision: =time_get='s =get= with a format and =do_get= with a
specifier, the C++11 interface, which take C++11's specification
(=ref-conformance.md=, "=time_get= with a format").

Fix: each entry's decision; a decision to keep writes its row in
=ref-conformance.md= chapter 2, and the comments then name it.

Check: every "extension" comment in =include= and =src= is either gone
or names its entry or its row in =ref-conformance.md=.

** TODO =ios_base::failure= constructs from a character pointer and from nothing

14882:2003 27.4.2.1.1 declares one constructor, ~explicit failure
(const string&)~. =__rw_failure=, which =ios_base::failure= names
(=include/rw/_iosbase.h= 57), adds ~explicit __rw_failure (const char*
= 0)~ (=include/rw/_iosfailure.h= 51-53, "extension"): a default
constructor and one from a pointer. 17.4.4.4, p2 permits both, and p3
holds: a call with a =string= behaves as before. The library throws
through the default constructor (=src/exception.cpp= 629-630).
=doc/stdlibref/ios-base--failure.html= shows the standard's
constructor only. No test. The =<stdexcept>= classes have the same
shape (below). Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as an addition 17.4.4.4 permits, or remove;
for the record, the library's throw path uses it. The decision on the
=<stdexcept>= default argument applies here too.

Check: the chapter 2 row exists and the comment at
=include/rw/_iosfailure.h= 51 names it.

** TODO =basic_filebuf= and =basic_stringbuf= override virtuals the standard does not list

14882:2003 27.8.1.1's synopsis lists the virtuals =basic_filebuf=
overrides, and 27.7.1's those of =basic_stringbuf=; neither lists
=xsputn=, and =basic_stringbuf='s lacks =showmanyc=. 17.4.4.4, p2's
latitude "does not extend to the implementation of virtual" functions.
The library overrides =xsputn= in both (=include/fstream= 199-201,
=include/sstream= 122) and =showmanyc= in =basic_stringbuf=
(=include/sstream= 124), "for efficiency". The difference shows in a
derived class: =basic_filebuf::xsputn= (=include/fstream.cc= 422)
writes to the file itself and never calls =overflow=, so a derived
=overflow= does not see =sputn='s characters.
=doc/stdlibref/basic-filebuf.html= and =basic-stringbuf.html= document
the =xsputn= overrides. No test pins the bypass. Removal costs bulk
output speed. The -1 the override returns on an error is its own
entry, below. Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as a departure, or remove the overrides; for
the record, the speed is the point and the derived =overflow= case is
narrow.

Check: the row exists.

** TODO The =short= and =int= extractors take the unsigned range in octal and hexadecimal

14882:2003 27.6.1.2.2 converts =operator>> (short&)= and =operator>>
(int&)= "as if" through a =num_get::get= on the type, a member the
2003 =num_get= lacks (22.2.2.1). N3242's 27.7.2.2.2, p2-3 settles it:
read a =long=, and set =failbit= and clamp outside the range of
=short= or =int=. The library reads a =long= and range-checks in
=__rw_check_overflow_short= and =__rw_check_overflow_int=
(=include/loc/_num_get.h= 240-322), called from =__rw_extract=
(=include/istream= 59-88). Under =oct=, =hex= or an empty =basefield=
a non-negative value is checked against =USHRT_MAX= and =UINT_MAX=:
"ffff" under =hex= stores ~short (-1)~ with =goodbit=, and on LP64
"ffffffff" stores ~int (-1)~. The decimal path and negative values
follow p2-3. The comments at 257 and 299 call the range "unreliable if
basefield is 0 and the sequence is decimal": by reading, "40000" under
an empty =basefield= stores ~short (-25536)~ with =goodbit=, which no
inserter writes. The helpers came from ~num_get::get (short&)~ and
~get (int&)~ (7c51b249); those members left in 444888d6 and the
helpers stayed for the extractors.

The case for the unsigned range is the inserter. =include/ostream=
234-260 formats a =short= or =int= under =oct= or =hex= through its
unsigned type, LWG 117's resolution (=ref-conformance.md= chapter 3),
so ~short (-1)~ goes out as "ffff" and the widening reads it back.
N3242's p2 does not, nor does libstdc++. =doc/stdlibref= does not
document it. No test pins it:
=tests/iostream/27.istream.fmat.arith.cpp= declares =HEX= and =OCT= at
430-431 and uses neither. Clamping as N3242 does breaks the round trip
of a negative =short= or =int= through =hex=; no caller in the library
depends on it. Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as a departure beside "Bump by =streamsize=",
the inverse of the LWG 117 inserter, or clamp as N3242 does; for the
record, clamping breaks a round trip the library's own output makes.
The decimal text under an empty =basefield= takes the signed range
either way.

Check: =27.istream.fmat.arith= rows: "ffff" under =hex= into =short=
as the decision says; "10000" under =hex= sets =failbit= and stores
=SHRT_MAX=; "40000" under an empty =basefield= sets =failbit=.

** TODO A temporary buffer is a per-thread static block, larger than asked, returned on its own thread

14882:2003 20.4.3, p1 obtains storage "sufficient to store up to n
adjacent T objects", p2 returns its address and capacity, and p3-4
deallocate any buffer =get_temporary_buffer= gave.
=src/tmpbuf.cpp= 45-50 keeps one 4096-byte buffer per thread
(=_RWSTD_THREAD=, =__thread= in the thread-safe builds), and
103-111 hand it out for any request up to that size with its whole
capacity: the first ~get_temporary_buffer<int> (2)~ on a thread
returns a capacity of 1024. A buffer returned on another thread
does not match that thread's block, and 89-92 pass it to
=__rw_deallocate=, =operator delete= on storage it never
allocated. =doc/stdlibref/get-temporary-buffer.html= 55-58
documents both: "at least n", and the return "from the same
thread". =tests/utilities/20.temp.buffer.cpp= 115 and 128 accept a
capacity of at least the request, and =20.temp.buffer.mt= returns
each buffer on its own thread. Without the block =stable_sort=,
=stable_partition= and =inplace_merge= allocate on every call.
Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as a departure, or allocate every buffer; for
the record, the per-thread block is the design and the reference
documents its limit. The row says that a return on another thread is
undefined here and defined by 20.4.3.

Check: a row in chapter 2 names =src/tmpbuf.cpp= and the same-thread
rule.

** TODO An environment variable selects a catalog for the library's exception texts

14882:2003 18.6.1, p8 makes =what ()= "an implementation-defined
NTBS", so the texts are the library's to choose. The library chooses
them at run time: the first library throw that formats a message opens
a catalog through =messages<char>= under the classic locale, named by
the environment variable =RWSTDERR= when set and =rwstderr:1=
otherwise, with an optional =:set= suffix (=include/rw/_error.h=
63-74, =src/exception.cpp= 490-550). A message found there formats the
=what ()= string; otherwise the built-in =_RWSTD_ERROR_STRINGS= do.
The original =README= (690) lists =etc/rwstderr.cat= among the
installed files. =doc/stdlibref= does not name the variable.
=18.exception= pins the built-in strings
(=tests/support/18.exception.cpp= 464); no test sets =RWSTDERR=.
Removing the catalog costs =src/rwstderr.msg=, its install line and
about sixty lines of =src/exception.cpp=; the built-in strings stay.
The reader writes into =getenv='s string, its own entry below. Found
by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record the catalog as the library's documentation of
an implementation-defined text, or remove it; for the record, 18.6.1,
p8 leaves the choice to the library.

Check: a chapter 2 row names =RWSTDERR=.

** TODO The standard headers bring =<pthread.h>= into a reentrant program

14882:2003 17.4.4.1, p1: "A C++ header may include other C++ headers."
=<pthread.h>= is not one. In a reentrant build (=_RWSTD_REENTRANT=,
which =-pthread='s =_REENTRANT= sets, =include/rw/_config.h= 81-88),
=<rw/_mutex.h>= includes =<rw/_mutex-pthread.h>= (51, 65), which
includes =<pthread.h>= (43) for the type of the mutex member of
=__rw_mutex_base=. =<string>= (through =<rw/_strref.h>= 40),
=<streambuf>= (40), =<istream>= (45) and every locale header (through
=<loc/_facet.h>= 36) reach it, so a program that includes =<string>=
sees =pthread_create=, =sched_yield= and the rest at global scope, and
one that declares such a name itself collides. =doc/stdlibref= says
nothing, and no test pins it. Hiding the header needs opaque storage
for the mutex and out-of-line lock calls: a layout and ABI change to
every class that holds a mutex. Found by the extension sweep's
reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as a departure, or hide the header behind
opaque storage; for the record, the mutex is a member of standard
classes by value, and other libraries on the platform make the same
trade.

Check: a chapter 2 row names =<pthread.h>= and the reentrant
condition.

** TODO =type_info= declares a protected constructor

14882:2003 18.5.1's synopsis gives =type_info= no constructor but the
private copy constructor. =include/typeinfo= 66-69 declare ~protected:
explicit type_info (const char*)~, "to prevent g++ warnings about
private ctor and no friends". A class derived from =type_info= can
name it. The library defines it nowhere (=src/typeinfo.cpp= defines
the destructor, =before=, =name= and the comparisons), so a call fails
to link unless the support library exports a definition.
=doc/stdlibref/typeinfo-h.html= shows the header synopsis only, and no
test names it. Removing it changes no layout and brings back the
warning it silences. Found by the extension sweep's reading,
2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record as a departure, or remove; for the record, it
is a declaration with no reachable effect, kept for a compiler
warning.

Check: =nm -D= of the 15D library and of =libstdc++= for
=_ZNSt9type_infoC2EPKc=, which settles whether a call can link.

** TODO =pair='s template assignment cites LWG 353, whose resolution is unchecked

=include/rw/_pair.h= 77-81 give =pair= a ~template <class U, class V>
pair& operator= (const pair<U, V>&)~ under the comment "lwg issue
353"; 14882:2003 20.2.2's synopsis has no assignment, and
=ref-conformance.md= chapter 3 lists 353 among the cited issues.
Hypothesis, by reading: if 353 closed without a change to the
standard, the member is an addition, permitted by 17.4.4.4, p2 but not
a resolution. Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the issue's status first; then the comment and chapter 3 follow
it, or the member takes a decision as an addition.

Check: the status of LWG 353 in the issues list.

** TODO =basic_ios= and =basic_ostream= declare type names the standard does not

14882:2003 27.4.4's synopsis gives =basic_ios= five member types, and
27.6.2.1 gives =basic_ostream= the same five. =basic_ios= adds
protected =streambuf_type= and =ostream_type=
(=include/rw/_basic_ios.h= 79-82), =basic_ostream= a public =ios_type=
(=include/ostream= 69). The names are not reserved, and 17.4.4.4
covers member functions, not types: a class derived from a stream that
uses one of these names unqualified, meaning a type of its own
namespace, finds the member first. The library uses them in
=basic_ios= and at =include/ostream= 126. =doc/stdlibref= documents
none of them on these pages. No test names them. The rename touches
two files. Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: rename to reserved names, or record; for the rename,
it costs two files and nothing outside them.

Check: no =ios_type=, =streambuf_type= or =ostream_type= in
=include/rw/_basic_ios.h= and =include/ostream=; a sanity build.

** TODO =basic_istream= has a public four-argument =read=

14882:2003 27.6.1.1's synopsis and 27.6.1.3 declare ~read (char_type*,
streamsize)~. =include/istream= 282-283 adds a public ~read
(char_type*, streamsize, int_type, int)~, defined at
=include/istream.cc= 278-357, whose last argument takes the flags of
=include/istream= 317-327: a program can call ~is.read (p, n, '\n',
0)~. 17.4.4.4, p2 permits the signature and p3 holds. The library
calls it from =ignore= (279) and from the =charT&= and =charT*=
extractors (490-510), non-members that need it accessible. Not in
=doc/stdlibref/basic-istream.html=; no test names it. The rename to a
reserved name touches four sites. Found by the extension sweep's
reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: rename to a reserved name, or record; for the rename,
it is an internal helper under a standard member's name.

Check: no four-argument =read= call outside =include/istream= and
=include/istream.cc=; a sanity build.

** TODO =auto_ptr<void>= is usable

14882:2003 20.4.5's synopsis declares ~X& operator* () const throw
()~, ill-formed for =X= =void= once instantiated, and 17.4.3.6, p2
makes instantiating a library template on an incomplete type
undefined. =include/rw/_autoptr.h= 43-77 define =__rw_nonvoid_ref= so
that ~operator*~ returns =void= for =void= (161), and =auto_ptr<void>=
compiles. Its destructor then deletes through a =void*=, which 5.3.5,
p3 and its footnote make undefined. The test calls it an extension:
=tests/utilities/20.auto.ptr.cpp= 221-262, run unless =void= is
disabled (374-377). =doc/stdlibref/auto-ptr.html= does not document
it. Nothing in the library instantiates =auto_ptr<void>=; the helper
also serves =tr1::shared_ptr<void>= (=include/tr1/_smartptr.h= 317).
Removal costs the test function. Found by the extension sweep's
reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: remove, ~operator*~ returning =element_type&= again,
or record; for removal, its purpose was =shared_ptr<void>= and the
class then deletes a =void*=.

Check: =20.auto.ptr= loses its =auto_ptr<void>= section and passes.

** TODO =uninitialized_copy= and =uninitialized_fill_n= take an allocator

14882:2003 20.4.4.1 and 20.4.4.3 declare ~uninitialized_copy (first,
last, result)~ and ~uninitialized_fill_n (first, n, x)~.
=include/rw/_specialized.h= 154-173 and 213-230, each under
"extension", add four-argument overloads in =namespace std= that
construct and destroy through an allocator. 17.4.4.3, p2 and footnote
171 permit additional non-member signatures. Callers:
=include/vector.cc= 268, 286 and 294. =doc/stdlibref= does not
document them, and no test calls them. Found by the extension sweep's
reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: move both into =namespace __rw= under reserved names,
or record; for the move, they are the vector's helpers and nothing
else names them.

Check: the two "extension" comments are gone, the three calls name
=__rw= functions, and the =23.vector.*= tests equal their pins.

** TODO =get_temporary_buffer= takes any distance type and a type tag

14882:2003 20.4.3 declares one ~get_temporary_buffer<T> (ptrdiff_t)~.
=include/rw/_rawiter.h= 123-154 add ~template <class T, class
Distance> pair<T*, Distance> get_temporary_buffer (Distance, T*)~ in
=namespace std=. Its comment gives two reasons: algorithms over
iterators whose difference type is not =ptrdiff_t=, and compilers
without partial specialization, a gate e9980bcd retired. Callers:
=include/algorithm.cc= 738-741, 1003-1006 and 1548-1551, each
commented "call an extension of get_temporary_buffer" for the second
reason. =doc/stdlibref/get-temporary-buffer.html= shows only the
standard form, and no test calls the overload. The three calls can
take ~get_temporary_buffer<T> (ptrdiff_t (n))~ or a =__rw= helper.
Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: move it into =namespace __rw=, or record; for the
move, only the algorithms call it, and the reason their comments give
is gone.

Check: the three "extension" comments in =algorithm.cc= are gone and
=25.sort=, =25.merge= and =25.partitions= equal their pins.

** TODO A null pointer with a count gives a string of uninitialized characters

14882:2003 21.3.1, p6: "Requires: s shall not be a null pointer and n
< npos." ~basic_string (const_pointer, size_type, const
allocator_type&)~ skips the copy for a null pointer and leaves =n=
characters uninitialized (=include/string.cc= 144-157, "extension").
The standard makes the call undefined, so no conforming program sees
it. One caller in the library relies on it: =bitset::to_string=
(=include/bitset= 438-439, "extension: allocate but do not
initialize"). No test; =doc/stdlibref= does not document it. The
neighbouring constructor asserts its pointer (=include/string.cc=
167). Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: remove the branch and assert as the neighbour does,
with =to_string= building its string another way, or record; for
removal, a program that relies on it reads indeterminate values.

Check: both comments are gone and =23.bitset= equals its pin.

** TODO =basic_string='s dispatch overloads and pointer-pair constructor are public

14882:2003 21.3's synopsis has =append=, =assign=, =insert= and
=replace= without extra arguments, and the template range constructor
only. The class declares, public, the targets of its
integral-or-iterator dispatch under the standard names: =append= with
a =void*= or an =int= last argument (=include/string= 288-298),
=assign= (328-339), =insert= (369-379) and the five-argument =replace=
overloads (491-553). It also declares a non-template ~basic_string
(const_pointer, const_pointer, const allocator_type&)~
(=include/string= 163-164, =include/string.cc= 179-192). 17.4.4.4, p2
permits added signatures and p3 holds for the standard's calls, but a
program can write ~s.append (3, 'x', 0)~, or construct from a =char*=
and a =const char*=, which the standard's template rejects for the
conflicting deduction. =doc/stdlibref/basic-string.html= lists none of
them, and no test names them. The dispatch targets are all called
through =_RWSTD_DISPATCH= inside the class; the template could replace
the pointer-pair constructor's direct copy with a =replace= call.
Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision in two parts: the dispatch targets take reserved
names, which costs nothing; the pointer-pair constructor stays as a
recorded addition, for the direct copy, or goes.

Check: the =21.string.*= rows are unchanged after the rename.

** TODO The =<stdexcept>= classes take a =const char*= that defaults to null

14882:2003 19.1.1 to 19.1.9 give each class one constructor, ~explicit
logic_error (const string& what_arg)~ and its kin, with the
postcondition ~strcmp (what (), what_arg.c_str ()) == 0~ (19.1.1, p3).
Each of the nine adds ~explicit X (const char *__s = 0)~, marked
"extension" (=include/stdexcept= 51-52, 65-66, 79-80, 93-94, 107-108,
121-122, 135-136, 149-150, 163-164): two additions. The =const char*=
constructor is C++11's 19.2.1, p4-5. The default argument gives every
class a default constructor, which no edition has: ~std::logic_error
e;~ compiles. 17.4.4.4, p2 lets an implementation add non-virtual
member signatures; whether that reaches a constructor, which has no
name (12.1, p1), is arguable.

The =const char*= constructor carries the header's design:
=<stdexcept>= declares =std::string= incomplete
(=include/rw/_exception.h= 48-57) and never defines it, so ~throw
std::runtime_error ("x")~ with no =<string>= in the translation unit
compiles only through it. The default argument carries the library's
own throws: =src/exception.cpp= 598-639 build ten exceptions as
~std::logic_error ()._C_assign (what, 0)~ and the like.
=doc/stdlibref/logic-error.html= and its eight siblings show the
=string= constructor only. No test default-constructs one;
=19.exceptions.mt= and =19.std.exceptions= construct from =const
char*=, which compiles through =string= once =<string>= is in, as it
is in both. Removing the default costs the ten sites and any program
that default-constructs; removing the =const char*= constructor breaks
every program that throws from a literal after =<stdexcept>= alone,
unless =<stdexcept>= includes =<string>=. Found by the extension
sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: keep the =const char*= constructor as a recorded
departure, C++11's text that the header leans on, and remove the
default argument, which is in no edition; or record both.
=ios_base::failure='s default argument (above) follows the same
decision.

Check: ~git grep -n "const char \*__s = 0" include~ is empty and the
library builds, =src/exception.cpp= passing an explicit null.

** TODO The =valarray= helpers have public constructors from the storage and a public =get_ref_mem_array=

14882:2003 26.3.5.1, p1, 26.3.7.1, p1, 26.3.8.1, p1 and 26.3.9.1 give
the four helpers no public constructors, and the synopses list no
accessor. =include/valarray= 1296, 1461, 1514 and 1621 declare public
constructors from an =__rw::__rw_array<T>*= and the selector, which a
program can call with a null pointer: ~std::slice_array<int> sa (0,
std::slice ());~ compiles. 1301, 1468, 1519 and 1627 declare a public
=get_ref_mem_array ()=, an unreserved name, that returns the
referenced storage. The name occurs 34 times in the header:
=valarray='s members (2081-2314) and the =__rw_unary_function= and
=__rw_binary_function= templates (1726-1819) call it. No reference
page and no test names them. The change costs a rename in those uses
and friend declarations for the constructors. Found by the extension
sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: the accessor takes a =_C_= name and the constructors
go private with =valarray<T>= a friend, or record; for the change,
both are plumbing.

Check: =git grep -n get_ref_mem_array include= is empty and the =26.*=
tests equal their pins.

** TODO =gslice= carries iteration state with three public members

14882:2003 26.3.6's synopsis: two constructors, =start ()=, =size ()=,
=stride ()=. =include/valarray= 1427-1434 add public =next_ind ()=,
=is_reseted ()= and =ind_numb ()=, "convenience functions", over two
state members (1442-1443); =next_ind= is defined in =src/valarray.cpp=
40-77 and exported (=src/mapfile.gcc= 937, =_ZNSt6gslice8next_indEv=).
A =gslice_array= steps its own copy through a =const_cast= in its
=const= members (=include/valarray= 1846 onward), and the copy
constructor resets the state. ~valarray<T>::operator[] (const gslice&)
const~ (2117-2135) steps the caller's =gslice= itself, through a
=const_cast=: a program's ~g.next_ind ()~ shifts what ~cv[g]~ reads,
and a =gslice= defined =const= is modified, which 7.1.5.1, p4 makes
undefined. No reference page documents them;
=tests/numerics/26.class.gslice.cpp= computes indices with its own
=next_index=. The rename changes the exported symbol, and
=mapfile.gcc= with it. Found by the extension sweep's reading,
2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: the three take =_C_= names, or record; for the
rename, they are the iteration engine of =gslice_array= and no program
should step it. The read through the caller's =gslice= has its own
entry, below.

Check: =nm -D= of the library shows no =gslice8next_ind=, and
=26.class.gslice= and =26.gslice.array.cassign= equal their pins.

** TODO A string buffer in input mode writes after its initial string

14882:2003 27.7.1.1, p3 and 27.7.1.2, p2 initialize "the output
sequence with the underlying sequence" and say nothing of where =pptr
()= starts. =_C_str= (=include/sstream.cc= 139-151) sets it to the end
when the mode has =in=, =app= or =ate=, and to the beginning
otherwise: ~std::stringstream ss ("abc"); ss << 'x';~ gives "abcx",
and an =ostringstream= on "abc" gives "xbc". a0d396e5 set the rule in
2006, citing LWG 562. N3242's 27.8.2.3, p3, 562 as the C++11 draft
printed it, reads "if mode & ios_base::in is true, pptr() == pbase() +
s.data()": the library's rule, with a typo. N3797's 27.8.2.3, p3
replaces =in= with =ate=. No edition positions on =app=. So the code
follows the first print of a resolution that was then corrected, and
adds =app=. =27.stringbuf.virtuals= pins the =in= rule: at
=tests/iostream/27.stringbuf.virtuals.cpp= 484, "abc" with ~in | out~
and "def" written leaves 6 read positions. =ref-conformance.md=
chapter 3 lists 562 among the cited issues without its effect. The
change moves that row and any that assume the end; a program that
appends to a =stringstream= built from a string would overwrite
instead. Found by the extension sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record the C++11 draft's reading, or follow N3797's
=ate= rule and drop =app=; for N3797, it is the reading every edition
since has kept.

Check: a =27.stringbuf.virtuals= row: "abc" in ~in | out~, ~sputc
('x')~, and =str ()= as the decision says, "xbc" or "abcx".

** TODO A program can replace or switch off every throw the library makes

14882:2003 17.4.4.8, p1: a library function reports a failure "by
throwing an exception of the type(s) described in their Throws:
paragraph"; 21.3.4, p3, for one, has =at= throw =out_of_range=. Every
throw the library makes goes through =__rw::__rw_throw=, from about 86
sites in =include= and =src=, most through =_RWSTD_REQUIRES=
(=include/rw/_defs.h= 702). =__rw_throw= calls through an exported
function pointer, =__rw::__rw_throw_proc= (=include/rw/_error.h= 57,
=src/exception.cpp= 646-648, =src/mapfile.gcc= 69), whose comment
invites a "user-defined handler (e.g., to prevent the library from
throwing exceptions or to implement logging)". A null pointer makes
=__rw_throw= return at once (=src/exception.cpp= 653-654), and a
handler that returns does the same. Either way the caller goes on past
the failed check: =basic_string::at= returns ~_C_data [__pos]~ for a
=__pos= past the end (=include/string= 945-951). A handler that throws
something else makes the library throw a type no Throws paragraph
names.

The names are reserved (17.4.3.1.2, p1) and =<rw/_error.h>= says it
"should never be #included directly by a program", but
=examples/manual/rwexcept.cpp= (35, 92) includes it and installs a
handler that prints and exits. =doc/stdlibref= says nothing. Three
tests use the hook: =18.exception= installs a returning handler and
checks the ids (=tests/support/18.exception.cpp= 438-629),
=18.support.dynamic= wraps the default one (378), and
=21.string.exceptions= installs one that throws the id as an =int=
(=tests/strings/21.string.exceptions.cpp= 66-72, 166). Removing the
hook costs those three test sections, the example and the map line;
the sites keep calling =__rw_throw=. Found by the extension sweep's
reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record the hook as a departure, the suite's seam for
observing the library's throws, reached only through a reserved name
in a header marked internal, and retire
=examples/manual/rwexcept.cpp=, which teaches programs to use it; or
remove the hook.

Check: a chapter 2 row names =__rw_throw_proc= and its null and
returning cases, or the hook is gone; either way
=examples/manual/rwexcept.cpp= is gone.

** TODO =getloc= on a non-const stream or buffer returns the object's own locale

14882:2003 27.4.2.3, p4 declares ~locale getloc () const~, which
returns the imbued locale, and 27.5.2.2.1, p4 does the same for
=basic_streambuf=. The library adds ~locale& getloc ()~ to both
(=include/rw/_iosbase.h= 225-230, =include/streambuf= 78-83), each
under an "extension" comment: it saves a reference count on every
formatted operation. 17.4.4.4, p2 permits an added member signature,
but p3 requires a call to a described signature to behave as if none
were added, and on a non-const object ~s.getloc ()~ now selects the
new overload. Two calls then behave differently. After ~const
std::locale &r = s.getloc (); s.imbue (l);~ the reference names =l=
where the standard's copy keeps the old locale. ~s.getloc () = l~
compiles in both, but the standard's assigns to a temporary and the
library's replaces the stream's locale without =imbue=: no
=imbue_event= callbacks (27.4.2.3, p1), no =pubimbue= of the buffer,
and on a buffer no call of the =imbue= virtual. =doc/stdlibref= does
not document the overload, and no test relies on it. 44 lines in 16
files under =include= and =src= call =getloc ()=; with the overload
gone those on non-const objects copy the locale, an atomic increment
and decrement in the thread-safe builds. Found by the extension
sweep's reading, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: remove both overloads and give the library's callers
a reserved accessor that returns the reference, or record; for
removal, the overload changes what a standard call does, and the
accessor keeps the saving.

Check: a =27.basic.ios= case that binds a ~const std::locale&~ to
~strm.getloc ()~, imbues another locale, and finds the reference still
equal to the first.

** TODO Every standard facet but =ctype= has a public destructor

The synopses of 14882:2003 22.2.1.1 through 22.2.7.2 declare every
facet's destructor protected, and 22.1.1.1.2 declares
=locale::facet='s protected too, its footnote 221 saying a facet need
not have a public destructor. The library declares it public in
=include/loc/=: =_codecvt.h= 142, 210, 359, 426, 440, 457;
=_collate.h= 65, 112, 158, 217, 241; =_messages.h= 86, 137;
=_moneypunct.h= 70, 213; =_money_put.h= 65; =_numpunct.h= 62, 174;
=_num_put.h= 61; =_time_get.h= 77, 197; =_time_put.h= 63, 108.
=num_get=, =money_get=, =ctype_byname<char>= and the primary =ctype=
and =ctype_byname= declare none, so theirs is implicit and public.
Only =ctype<char>=, =ctype<wchar_t>= and =ctype_byname<wchar_t>=
follow the standard (=_ctype.h= 290, 491, 623). A program can define a
facet as an automatic or static object, ~std::num_put<char> np;~, or
=delete= one through its own type; under the standard's access both
are ill-formed. From the 2005 import (e6c7726b).

=doc/stdlibref= shows the standard's access where it shows a
destructor (=collate.html= 107, =codecvt.html= 131). No test pins the
access, but tests use it: =22.locale.num.put.cpp= 495 and 2216,
=22.locale.codecvt.cpp= 330, 341, 2596 and 2703,
=22.locale.codecvt.do_out.cpp= 480, 551 and 554, and
=22.locale.codecvt.length.cpp= 196, 250 and 253 define facets as
objects. The library does not: its ordinary facets are
placement-constructed in static buffers and never destroyed
(=src/use_facet.h= 105-110, 126-131), and =__rw_locale= deletes the
others through =__rw_facet*= as its friend (=src/locale_body.cpp= 366,
426, 445). The Itanium ABI does not mangle access, so the change costs
GCC and Clang builds no symbol. A program that defines a standard
facet as an object breaks. Found by the extension sweep's reading,
2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: the destructors protected as =ctype='s are, or
record; for the change, the access is the standard's interface, it
costs the library nothing, and the programs it breaks would not
compile against libstdc++ either.

Check: the library and the localization tests compile with every facet
destructor protected, once the twelve test sites derive from the facet
or install it in a locale.

** TODO =include/tr1= ships part of TR1 as installable headers

14882:2003 17.4.1.2, p2 and Table 11 list the 33 C++ headers; none is
under =tr1/=, and the target is the C++03 library
(=ref-conformance.md= chapter 0). =include/tr1= holds four headers,
1435 lines, in =std::tr1=:

- =<tr1/cstdint>=: C99's integer types and limit macros, with
  =int_least128_t= and its kin where the configuration finds the type
  (312-317).
- =<tr1/stdint.h>=: the same types brought to global scope (34-97).
- =<tr1/array>=: =array=, its comparisons and =swap=. =tuple_size= and
  =tuple_element= are declared "defined in <tr1/tuple>", a header the
  tree does not have, and the two =get= overloads have no body
  (341-358): ~get<0> (a)~ compiles and fails to link.
- =<tr1/_smartptr.h>=: =shared_ptr=, =weak_ptr=, =bad_weak_ptr= and
  the casts. No =<tr1/memory>= includes it, so a program reaches it
  only by a reserved-looking file name. ~shared_ptr (auto_ptr<U>&)~
  (232-234) and =enable_shared_from_this= (602-603) are declared
  without a definition, and the stream inserter is under ~#if 0~
  (429-436).

No other header in =include= and no file in =src= includes them. Three
tests do, pinned in all six baseline tables:
=tests/tr1.util/2.smartptr.shared= (121 assertions in 15D),
=2.smartptr.weak= (45) and =tests/tr1.c99/8.cstdint= (157); none
covers =array=. =make install= copies them with the rest of =include=
(=GNUmakefile= 634). =doc/stdlibref= and =ref-conformance.md= do not
mention TR1. =_smartptr.h= is the one user of =__rw_nonvoid_ref=
besides =auto_ptr= (above). The =_RWSTD_INT*_T= characterizations stay
either way: =src/random.cpp= and others read them. Removal costs four
headers, two test directories, three rows in each baseline table and
any installed program that includes a =tr1/= header. Found by two of
the extension sweep's readings, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: remove =include/tr1= and its two test directories, or
record TR1 as a partial addition; for removal, it is a later library's
interface, unfinished, with declarations that link to nothing.

Check: =include/tr1=, =tests/tr1.util= and =tests/tr1.c99= are gone,
and the six pins lose exactly those three rows.

** TODO Racing =locale::global= calls can split the C and C++ locales

=locale::global= (=src/locale_global.cpp= 59-72) calls =setlocale= and
