** TODO =vector<bool>='s range =insert= reads an input range twice

=include/vector.cc= 562 takes ~std::distance (first, last)~ over the
range and then copies it (565 or 574), so an input iterator range is
spent on the count and the copy reads nothing. 14882:2003 24.1.1, p3:
"Algorithms on input iterators should never attempt to pass through
the same iterator twice." Seen in passing by the extension sweep,
2026-11-04.

Fix: the range =insert= reads an input range once, into a temporary or
element by element.

Check: =insert= at =begin ()= from an =istream_iterator<bool>= over "1
0 1" inserts three elements.

** TODO =valarray::sum= adds with ~operator+~ from =T ()=

14882:2003 26.3.2.7, p2 computes =sum= "by applying ~operator+=~ to a
copy of an element of the array and all other elements".
=include/valarray= 167-169 return ~accumulate (begin, end, value_type
())~, which starts from =T ()= and applies ~operator+~: a =T= with
~+=~ and no ~+~ does not compile, and one whose =T ()= is not an
additive identity gives another sum. Seen in passing by the extension
sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: =sum= copies the first element and applies ~+=~ for the rest.

Check: =sum= of a =valarray= over a type with ~+=~ alone compiles, and
over one whose =T ()= is 1 gives the elements' sum.

** TODO A second =cv[g]= with one =gslice= may read its start twice

=next_ind= at the end of its walk wraps, sets =_C_reset= and returns
=start=, and the next call clears the flag and returns =start= again
(=src/valarray.cpp= 40-77). ~valarray<T>::operator[] (const gslice&)
const~ (=include/valarray= 2117-2135) calls it =ind_numb ()= times on
the caller's =gslice= and never takes the wrapping call, so the state
stays at the last index. Hypothesis, by reading and a hand trace over
a 2-by-3 =gslice=: a second ~cv[g]~ with the same =g= reads =start=
twice and drops the last element. The =gslice_array= paths take the
wrap in their loop condition and start clean. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the const subscript steps a copy of the =gslice=, as
=gslice_array= does.

Check: ~a = cv[g]; b = cv[g];~ for a 2-by-3 =gslice=, with =a= and =b=
equal element by element.

** TODO The =random_shuffle= generator's warm-up may run four table entries

=src/random.cpp= 87-91: the warm-up loop indexes ~__rw_table [i]~
inside the loop over =ii=, so it runs entries 0 to 3 fifty-five times
each where the subtractive generator it follows runs all fifty-five
four times. The line is unchanged since the 2005 import. Hypothesis,
by reading: the inner index is meant to be =ii=. A fix moves every
result and =25.random.shuffle='s expected array. Seen in passing by
the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the inner loop runs over ~__rw_table [ii]~, then the test's
expected array.

Check: the loop read against Knuth's subtractive generator or
Numerical Recipes' =ran3=.

** TODO The standard objects are references, which a template argument can tell

=include/iostream= 55-62 declare the eight objects through
=_RWSTD_IOSTREAM_OBJECT= as references, and =src/iostream.cpp= 74-78
call the departure from 27.3 undetectable. 14882:2003 14.3.2, p1 takes
as a template argument the address of an object with external linkage.
Hypothesis, by reading: ~template <std::ostream&> struct S;~ cannot be
instantiated on =std::cout=, since a reference is not an object. Seen
in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record the departure in chapter 2, or define the
objects as objects.

Check: compile ~S<std::cout>~.

** TODO =_C_opfx= skips the tie's flush on a loop of distinct buffers

=include/ostream= 270-284 skip ~tie ()->flush ()~ when the tie chain
loops or a stream in it shares the buffer. Footnote 292 of 14882:2003
27.6.2.3 allows the skip where no synchronization is necessary, which
holds for a shared buffer but not for a loop over distinct buffers.
The loop exists because =flush= builds a sentry (LWG 581); 2003's
27.6.2.6, p7 says =flush= does not behave as an unformatted output
function. Hypothesis, by reading: two streams on distinct buffers tied
to each other do not flush each other. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: flush each distinct buffer of the loop once, or
record the skip.

Check: two streams on distinct buffers, tied to each other: an
insertion into one flushes the other.

** TODO =<stdlib.h>= and =<math.h>= leave the C++ overloads out of the global namespace

14882:2003 D.5, p2 has each =name.h= place the =cname= header's names
in the global namespace. =include/rw/_config.h= 97-106 defines
=_RWSTD_NO_DEPRECATED_C_HEADERS= unless a program asks for the pure
headers, and under it =include/ansi/stdlib.h= and =math.h= include the
C library's header and nothing more (=stdlib.h= 73-74). Hypothesis, by
reading: under glibc =<stdlib.h>= then gives no ~::abs (long)~ and
=<math.h>= no ~::sin (float)~, so ~abs (-1L)~ after =<stdlib.h>= calls
~abs (int)~. Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: the =name.h= headers bring the =cname= overloads to
global scope, or record the C library's set.

Check: ~sizeof (abs (0L))~ after =<stdlib.h>= alone.

** TODO An installed =config.h= shares its name with the programs'

=include/rw/_config.h= 36 includes =<config.h>=, a name many programs'
build systems use, and =make install= copies the library's to the top
of =$(PREFIX)/include= (=GNUmakefile= 633). Hypothesis, by reading: a
program whose own =config.h= is earlier on the search path configures
the library with it, and one whose own is later gets the library's.
Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: a name of the library's own, under =rw/=, or record.

Check: ~#include <string>~ with =-I= of a directory holding an empty
=config.h= ahead of the library's.

** TODO =type_info= compares by name where GCC marks a name to compare by address

In 15D under GCC =_RWSTD_NO_TYPE_INFO_BEFORE=, =_EQUALITY= and =_NAME=
are defined (=config.h= 484-487), so =src/typeinfo.cpp= 87-130
compiles =before=, =name= and the comparisons. =name= returns the raw
mangled name and equality compares names with =strcmp=; GCC's support
library skips a leading =*=, its mark for a name to compare by
address. Hypothesis, by reading: =typeid= of an anonymous-namespace
type gives a =name ()= that starts with =*=, and two such types of one
name in two translation units compare equal. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: per the check: =name= skips the mark and a marked name compares
by address, as the support library does.

Check: those two calls in a two-file program.

** TODO The range =splice= checks 23.2.2.4 p12 with the wrong iterators

The range =splice= (=include/list.cc=) asserts
~__i != __j && __i != __k~ when the source is the list itself, and in
a debug build walks ~[__j, __k)~ asserting ~__i != __j~ at every
step, an invariant of the loop. 14882:2003 23.2.2.4 p12 leaves the
result undefined only for a position in ~[first, last)~: a position
inside the range past =first= is never caught, and a position equal
to =last=, which the paragraph allows and which changes nothing,
fails the assertion; a release build returns at once. From the 2005
import. Seen in passing while retiring the node buffer, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the loop asserts ~__i != __it~, and the first assertion drops
~__i != __k~.

Check: ~l.splice (l.end (), l, l.begin (), l.end ())~ runs in 15D; a
position inside the range fails the assertion.

